SECTION 4 - LAB 2 : SPLIT HORIZON + FULL MESH
===========================================================

Objective
---------

Understand iBGP split horizon and
why full mesh exists.


Topology
--------

Physical:

R1 -------- R2 -------- R3


Split Horizon Behavior
----------------------

R1 advertises:

1.1.1.1


R2 learns:

1.1.1.1


Question:

Will R2 advertise to R3?


Answer:

NO


Reason:

Routes learned from one iBGP peer
are NOT advertised to another iBGP peer.


Result:

R3 cannot learn 1.1.1.1


Why Full Mesh Is Needed
-----------------------

Required peerings:

R1 ↔ R2

R2 ↔ R3

R1 ↔ R3


This creates:

Full mesh iBGP


Session Formula
---------------

Sessions = n(n-1)/2


3 routers:

3 sessions


10 routers:

45 sessions


Problem:

Full mesh scales poorly.


Important Rule
--------------

iBGP preserves next-hop.


Fixes for next-hop issues:

- next-hop-self
- static routes
- IGP reachability


===========================================================
SECTION 5 - LAB 3 : ROUTE REFLECTOR
===========================================================

Problem
-------

Full mesh becomes difficult to scale.


Solution
--------

Route Reflector


Topology
--------

        R2 (RR)

       /       \

R1(Client)   R3(Client)


Operation
---------

Client sends route.

RR learns route.

RR reflects route.


Result
------

Clients do not need direct peering.


Important Command
-----------------

neighbor X route-reflector-client


Additional Feature
------------------

cluster-id

Used for loop prevention.


